WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.001
The Falkland Islands: twelve thousand square kilometres of ancient rock in the cold

00:00:05.001 --> 00:00:05.842
South Atlantic.

00:00:06.942 --> 00:00:10.622
They lie about five hundred kilometres east of southern South America.

00:00:12.942 --> 00:00:17.750
Unlike every other island in this course, the Falklands are built of continental crust.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:22.337
The oldest rocks, in the far south of West Falkland, are about a billion years old.

00:00:22.337 --> 00:00:24.879
Above them lie hard quartzites and sandstones,

00:00:24.879 --> 00:00:29.079
and then sediments left by an ice age about three hundred million years ago.

00:00:30.179 --> 00:00:33.311
That sequence closely matches the rocks of South Africa.

00:00:33.311 --> 00:00:38.176
The leading explanation is that the Falklands block lay off south-eastern Africa within

00:00:38.176 --> 00:00:41.419
the supercontinent Gondwana, broke away as Gondwana split,

00:00:41.419 --> 00:00:46.116
and rotated about one hundred and eighty degrees on its way to its present position.

00:00:46.116 --> 00:00:50.030
Evidence from the magnetism of ancient rock supports a large rotation,

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.099
though the details are still debated.

00:00:53.199 --> 00:00:56.240
Today the islands sit on a shallow continental plateau.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.607
Watch what happens when we lower the sea by one hundred and twenty-five metres,

00:01:00.607 --> 00:01:02.708
roughly the level of the last ice age.
